  "caveats": [
    "These are isolated materialized relation phases, not complete queries. Storage, SQL resolution, grouping, sorting and output projection are excluded.",
    "The accounted filter reseeds its account and owner before each invocation in both controls; fixture byte sizing/account construction are outside timing.",
    "Values/errors, filter row identity/order, every small-case statement/lifetime failure prefix and rejected-row memory release agree before/after timing and across runtimes.",
    "Warmup checkpoints follow semantic validation; first-call/compile latencies include runtime warmup effects and are distinct from warmed measurements.",
    "Ruby 4.0.7 and 4.1.0-preview1 differ; cross-runtime comparisons do not isolate YJIT versus ZJIT alone.",
    "The warmed main ZJIT diagnostic observes 64 live kernels after the inner population helper returns; interpreter/YJIT observe 0. All original observations are preserved. A separate fresh process for each runtime observes 0 kernels after the complete outer churn helper returns and after a completed population thread exits, across three full collections. The fresh ZJIT probe also observes 0 after its inner helper, so this probe does not reproduce or uniquely identify the root behind the original 64. These results are consistent with VM/JIT execution-state liveness rather than a generated kernel capturing an engine or cache; they do not establish immediate reclamation in every call context.",
    "The separate two-batch probe creates 512 kernels in total. After thread exit the interpreter retains 0 anonymous kernel classes, YJIT 21, and ZJIT 512 with 9,133,168 reported JIT bytes, despite 0 live kernel instances. The engine source/pool LRU bounds retained instances to 64; it does not bound VM class or machine-code retention. No claim of an overall JIT memory cap is made."
  ],
